[A839] round 3, item 4: SMT-aware local booking. A PATCH for the manager to review and apply; not applied.

Apply from $MANAGEMENT/2026-09-23:   patch -p1 --dry-run < cicd-a839/round3/smt-booking.patch
(dry-run checked against the live files, md5 cpu_ranges.py c5ea50e8, native-shards.py f27413f0, start-act.py 57c38e74)

WHY. Round 2 measured that a Verilator simulation whose SMT sibling runs another simulation needs ~2.3x the
cycles for the same instructions (milan_dp's gPTP leg: 2.50 T instructions in 1.14-1.22 T cycles with its core to
itself, 2.79 T with a busy sibling). This host's siblings are 2k and 2k+1 (sysfs thread_siblings_list, read by the
patch, not assumed).

WHAT.
1. cpu_ranges.py
   - threads(<range>) and `cpu_ranges.py threads <range>`: one CPU per physical core of a booked range (the lower
     sibling), and a refusal for a range that splits a core. 56-63 -> 56,58,60,62.
   - busy() counts both siblings of every CPU a unit is pinned to (whole_cores). A unit pinned to 56,58,60,62
     holds 56-63, so no other booking lands on 57. Every existing booking is an aligned 8/12/16 range, so whole
     cores already, and nothing that books today changes.
   - unpinned(), printed by `show`: the active milan-* units whose main process keeps the full 0-95 mask (the
     queue daemons excepted). busy() cannot count them (they would make every CPU busy), and their work runs on
     every core, booked or not.
2. native-shards.py: each <cpus-k> must be whole cores; the shard unit is pinned to threads(<cpus-k>) with
   MAKEFLAGS=-j<cores>. An 8-CPU booking (4 cores) keeps -j4, as today, on 4 threads instead of 8.
3. start-act.py: refuses a range that splits a core. The act job containers keep their whole range. Their CPU
   budget step caps simulations and campaigns at 4 (SIM_JOBS, CI_CAMPAIGN_JOBS) on 8 cores, so each simulation can
   already have a core of its own, and the builds keep both threads.
Not covered: the CPU budget rounds and gate units launched by other scripts (run-when-cpus.sh, daily-area.sh,
prepare-consumer.py, the 2026-09-22 launchers). They book through cpu_ranges and can adopt threads() the same way.

MEASURED BEFORE/AFTER ON ONE LOCAL SHARD (2026-11-24 17:45-18:20, head f6c11a25's tree, shard 8/19 =
capture_coherence__1 + maap__0, `run_all_suites.sh --ci-pieces --no-preflight`, hosted budgets (4 jobs), no
ccache, SUITE_TIMEOUT=7200, two clones run concurrently):
   before  2 cores, both siblings used (84-87), the 4-CPU hosted shape:  3102.1 s wall, 3101.6 CPU-s,
           10.97 T cycles, 13.75 T instructions, IPC 1.25
   after   4 cores booked (72-75, 88-91), one thread each (72,74,88,90):  3256.7 s wall, 3114.7 CPU-s,
           11.01 T cycles, 13.75 T instructions, IPC 1.25
   both:   2/2 PASS; capture_coherence__1 (184 lines) and maap__0 (282 lines) logs IDENTICAL
RESULT: no gain on this host today, and the counters say why. The same instructions took the same cycles in both
arms, so the "idle" siblings were not idle: `cpu_ranges.py show` with this patch lists ten unpinned units (seven
sessions launched with taskset -c 0-95, outside lane-queue's session_pool(), and gate units:
lwsrppin-a834-r4-h-1x1, lwsrppin-a834-r4-h-8x8-1if, qual1124-a840-chain8), and every CPU read 100 % busy (5 s
/proc/stat sample, load 195-230). Their threads ran on the siblings this patch leaves idle. pool-spill.sh, which
widens every pinned process to 0-95, was not running (its last action was 2026-11-18), and every pin held.
So the patch is necessary and not sufficient. Its gain (round 2's 2.3x cycles per simulation) appears only once
nothing floats: confine every session to session_pool() and every gate unit to a booking, then re-measure. The
units to fix are the ones `show` prints.

