+2026-11-24 (A839 round 3): a Verilator simulation whose SMT sibling runs another simulation needs ~2.3x the cycles for
+the same instructions (milan_dp's gPTP leg: 1.14-1.22 T cycles with its core to itself, 2.79 T with a busy sibling). So
+a booking is whole physical cores (this host's siblings are 2k and 2k+1, read from sysfs, not assumed), and a simulation
+unit runs on one thread of each booked core (`threads`), its sibling booked and left idle. busy() therefore counts both
+siblings of every CPU a unit is pinned to: a unit on 56,58,60,62 holds 56-63, and no other booking lands on 57.
+An idle sibling stays idle only if nothing floats onto it: a unit whose main process keeps the full 0-95 mask is not
+counted as busy (it is a daemon to busy()) and its work runs on every core, booked or not. On 2026-11-24 ten active
+units held that mask, sessions started outside lane-queue's session_pool() and gate units among them, and a shard run
+on four booked cores, one thread each, ran no faster than on two cores' four threads (cycles 11.01 T vs 10.97 T).
+`show` therefore names them, so each can be confined to the session pool or a booking.
+
 usage: cpu_ranges.py pick [--width 16|12|8]  -> prints a free aligned range (default: pick8) or exits 1
        cpu_ranges.py show                   -> prints the busy set and the free ranges
+       cpu_ranges.py threads <range>        -> prints one CPU per physical core of a booked range (refuses a split core)
